It IS a functionality of touch; it is deliberately programmed into
touch for that purpose. The first three sentences of the touch manual
(from "info touch"):

   `touch' changes the access and/or modification times of the
specified
   files.  Synopsis:

        touch [OPTION]... FILE...

      Any FILE that does not exist is created empty.


It was put there so that the outcome of "touch filename" is
predictable: after execution there WILL be a file there and it WILL
have a current lastmodified timestamp, and if the file existed before
executing touch its contents will not have been damaged. This is
something that is useful for controlling "make", and it is an idiom
that is not easy to write in the shell without an if statement.

There is even a specific commandline argument, "-c", that will prevent
touch from creating files.



